    "turn_text": "I didn't, you know, the good Lord works in mysterious ways. He opens doors. And so my last semester in law school, I moved back home to do an internship at the attorney's office in Rankin and Madison County. And as that internship was coming to an end, there was a young lady in our office who was going out on maternity leave. And so I was able to stay on on a part time basis to kind of help make sure that her caseload was being handled while she was out and also studying for the bar and so was able to pass the bar while she was out on maternity leave when she came back. I then went to my boss at that time john kitchens and said hey I wouldn't like a full time job with you and he said look I'd love to hire you, but I don't have an open position and so I told him and I asked him I said if I can find some funding would you use that funding. to allow me to stay on full time. And he told me that he would. And so I began my very brief lobbying career. I lobbied the local ranking county board supervisors for some additional funding. I was able to get that. And I wasn't the best lobbyist. They only were agreed to raise, I think, the amount of the budget by $24,000. So $2,000 a month. But at that time, I was able to scrape by on that amount. And really thought, sure, that I would stay in the DA's office my entire life. Really love what I did and love working with law enforcement with our judicial, our court systems, going into court room, advocating on behalf of victims. And really felt like that that was going to be my career path, that I would just be a lifelong prosecutor. But when Greg Harper, who was a friend of mine who previously served in this position, retired, I created an opportunity that I had never really thought about. And so Haley and I talked about it and we prayed about it and kind of got a piece that this was the direction that we were being led and have been fortunate now to serve two terms. And so it's such an honor to represent, you know, the people of Mississippi and our nation's capital for Haley and I mean, you know, Mississippi is just such a special place. And, you know, if you look around, I mean, this is where We grew up. This is where our children are growing up. This is home for us. And to have that honor is truly amazing. And so our goal each and every day when we get up is to make sure that we're representing the people of Mississippi in a manner that they'll be proud when when ultimately our day is done and somebody else is in this office. And so we want to continue to do that. And look, the fact that we get to represent our Mississippi State University that is in the third district And so not only am I alumni of the university, but I get to advocate on behalf of the university major in Washington, DC, get to work with Dr. Keenan and his incredible staff. That's something special. And so, and I hope that the university knows that they've got an alumni who's in Washington, DC, not only fighting for Mississippi, but fighting for the Beach of Art University.",
